text:
VAR is a multivariate1 linear time series model, designed to capture the joint dynamics2 of multiple time series through an interconnected system.
VARs, by default, treat each endogenous variable3 in the system as a function of lagged values of all endogenous variables. Exogenous variables enter only when explicitly added.
VARs offer a simple4 and flexible alternative to traditional multiple equation models5.


3.1.1 A closer look: joint dynamics, interconnected system and endogeneity
In a VAR the variables are not studied side by side. They form a single system, where each one drives the others and is driven by them. This happens because each variable appears on the left-hand side of its own equation and on the right-hand side of the others. Since every variable has its own equation, each one is explained within the system, and none can be treated as exogenous: this is exactly what endogeneity means here.
The link is not a one-off cross-effect, nor does it run in one direction only: it is mutual, and it propagates forward indefinitely.
Example:
To fix ideas, let inflation (\(\pi\)) and GDP each depend on both their own lag and the other’s lag. Since the true data-generating process is unknown, the relationship is left as a generic function:
\[\begin{aligned}
\pi_t &= f(\pi_{t-1},\, gdp_{t-1}) \\
gdp_t &= f(gdp_{t-1},\, \pi_{t-1})
\end{aligned}\]
Following a single cross-channel makes the mechanism visible and more intuitive. Take the \(gdp \to \pi\) link:
\[gdp_{t-1} \;\rightarrow\; \pi_t \;\rightarrow\; gdp_{t+1} \;\rightarrow\; \pi_{t+2} \;\rightarrow\; gdp_{t+3} \;\rightarrow\; \cdots\]
This single thread is real, but on its own it understates the structure. Own lags run alongside these cross-links, and each own lag is itself shaped by the other variable one period earlier: \(gdp_{t-1}\) already carries \(\pi_{t-2}\). Every value is therefore fed, at every period, by both variables at once; as shown in the animation below, where horizontal arrows are own lags (persistence) and diagonal arrows are cross-lags (spillovers).
This self-sustaining feedback loop is what makes the system genuinely joint, and it is the aspect most often underestimated when first learning VAR.

{
  const GDP = 0, PI = 1;
  const periods = ["t\u22121", "t", "t+1", "t+2"];
  const N = periods.length;

  const colGDP = "#4c78a8", colPI = "#e45756";
  const faint = "#ececec", firedCol = "#f6c667", hotArrow = "#f0a202";
  const coldFill = "#f5f5f5", coldStroke = "#cfcfcf";
  const colOf = r =&gt; (r === GDP ? colGDP : colPI);

  const width = 680, height = 300;
  const mL = 88, mR = 30;
  const dx = (width - mL - mR) / (N - 1);
  const legendY = 22, periodY = 58, yTop = 104, yBot = 224, captionY = 288;
  const R = 15;
  const x = i =&gt; mL + i * dx;
  const yOf = r =&gt; (r === GDP ? yTop : yBot);

  const frames = [
    {type:"start", caption:"Start at t\u22121: GDP and \u03C0 are both given."},
    {type:"trans", col:0, caption:"Both feed forward: own lags (horizontal) and cross-lags (diagonal) fire together."},
    {type:"trans", col:1, caption:"The same pattern repeats: t feeds t+1 through all four channels."},
    {type:"trans", col:2, caption:"\u2026and again, the feedback loop sustains itself, forward without end."},
    {type:"hold",  caption:"Every value is fed by both variables: one joint, interconnected system."},
    {type:"hold",  caption:"Every value is fed by both variables: one joint, interconnected system."}
  ];
  const total = frames.length;

  function stateAt(f) {
    const hot = [Array(N).fill(false), Array(N).fill(false)];
    hot[GDP][0] = true;
    hot[PI][0]  = true;
    const fired = new Set();
    let current = [];
    for (let idx = 1; idx &lt;= f && idx &lt; total; idx++) {
      const fr = frames[idx];
      if (fr.type !== "trans") continue;
      const c = fr.col;
      const arrowsThis = [];
      for (let r = 0; r &lt; 2; r++) {
        if (hot[r][c]) {
          arrowsThis.push([c, r, c + 1, GDP]);
          arrowsThis.push([c, r, c + 1, PI]);
        }
      }
      hot[GDP][c + 1] = true; hot[PI][c + 1] = true;
      if (idx === f) current = arrowsThis;
      else arrowsThis.forEach(a =&gt; fired.add(a.join(",")));
    }
    return {hot, fired, current, caption: frames[f].caption};
  }

  const container = d3.create("div").style("font-family", "system-ui, sans-serif");
  const controls = container.append("div")
    .style("display", "flex").style("gap", "6px").style("align-items", "center")
    .style("margin-bottom", "6px");
  const mkBtn = (label) =&gt; controls.append("button").text(label)
    .style("padding", "3px 10px").style("font-size", "13px")
    .style("border", "1px solid #ccc").style("border-radius", "6px")
    .style("background", "#fff").style("cursor", "pointer");
  const playBtn = mkBtn("Pause");
  const resetBtn = mkBtn("Reset");
  const prevBtn = mkBtn("\u2039");
  const nextBtn = mkBtn("\u203A");
  const holder = container.append("div");

  let f = 0, playing = true;

  function drawArrow(svg, i0, r0, i1, r1, mode) {
    const x0 = x(i0), y0 = yOf(r0), x1 = x(i1), y1 = yOf(r1);
    const a = Math.atan2(y1 - y0, x1 - x0);
    const sx = x0 + R * Math.cos(a), sy = y0 + R * Math.sin(a);
    const ex = x1 - (R + 5) * Math.cos(a), ey = y1 - (R + 5) * Math.sin(a);
    const col = mode === "hot" ? hotArrow : mode === "fired" ? firedCol : faint;
    const w = mode === "hot" ? 3.5 : mode === "fired" ? 2.5 : 1.2;
    svg.append("line")
      .attr("x1", sx).attr("y1", sy).attr("x2", ex).attr("y2", ey)
      .attr("stroke", col).attr("stroke-width", w)
      .attr("opacity", mode === "faint" ? 0.6 : 1)
      .attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-" + mode + ")");
  }

  function update() {
    const st = stateAt(f);
    holder.selectAll("*").remove();
    const svg = holder.append("svg")
      .attr("viewBox", [0, 0, width, height])
      .attr("width", width)
      .attr("style", "max-width:100%;height:auto;");

    const defs = svg.append("defs");
    [["faint", faint], ["fired", firedCol], ["hot", hotArrow]].forEach(([id, c]) =&gt; {
      defs.append("marker").attr("id", "ah-" + id)
        .attr("viewBox", "0 0 10 10").attr("refX", 9).attr("refY", 5)
        .attr("markerWidth", 7).attr("markerHeight", 7).attr("orient", "auto")
        .append("path").attr("d", "M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z").attr("fill", c);
    });

    // row baselines + labels (pi bigger)
    [["GDP", GDP, 16], ["\u03C0", PI, 26]].forEach(([lab, r, fs]) =&gt; {
      svg.append("line").attr("x1", mL - 20).attr("x2", width - mR)
        .attr("y1", yOf(r)).attr("y2", yOf(r)).attr("stroke", "#f2f2f2");
      svg.append("text").attr("x", mL - 34).attr("y", yOf(r) + fs / 3)
        .attr("text-anchor", "end").attr("font-weight", "bold")
        .attr("font-size", fs).attr("fill", colOf(r)).text(lab);
    });

    periods.forEach((p, i) =&gt; {
      svg.append("text").attr("x", x(i)).attr("y", periodY)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 12).attr("fill", "#999").text(p);
    });

    // faint full lattice
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; N - 1; i++) {
      drawArrow(svg, i, GDP, i + 1, GDP, "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, PI,  i + 1, PI,  "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, GDP, i + 1, PI,  "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, PI,  i + 1, GDP, "faint");
    }
    // already-fired
    st.fired.forEach(k =&gt; {
      const a = k.split(",").map(Number);
      drawArrow(svg, a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3], "fired");
    });
    // current transition: all arrows together
    st.current.forEach(a =&gt; drawArrow(svg, a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3], "hot"));

    // nodes
    for (let r = 0; r &lt; 2; r++) {
      for (let i = 0; i &lt; N; i++) {
        const isHot = st.hot[r][i];
        svg.append("circle").attr("cx", x(i)).attr("cy", yOf(r)).attr("r", R)
          .attr("fill", isHot ? colOf(r) : coldFill)
          .attr("stroke", isHot ? colOf(r) : coldStroke).attr("stroke-width", 2);
      }
    }

    // gentle highlight of both starting values at t-1 (no shock language)
    if (frames[f].type === "start") {
      [GDP, PI].forEach(r =&gt; {
        svg.append("circle").attr("cx", x(0)).attr("cy", yOf(r)).attr("r", R + 5)
          .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", colOf(r)).attr("stroke-width", 2)
          .attr("opacity", 0.55);
      });
    }

    // legend (raised)
    const lx = width - mR - 250, ly = legendY;
    svg.append("line").attr("x1", lx).attr("y1", ly).attr("x2", lx + 24).attr("y2", ly)
      .attr("stroke", "#bbb").attr("stroke-width", 2).attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-faint)");
    svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + 30).attr("y", ly + 4).attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#666").text("own lag");
    svg.append("line").attr("x1", lx + 92).attr("y1", ly - 6).attr("x2", lx + 116).attr("y2", ly + 6)
      .attr("stroke", "#bbb").attr("stroke-width", 2).attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-faint)");
    svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + 122).attr("y", ly + 4).attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#666").text("cross-lag");

    // caption + counter
    svg.append("text").attr("x", width / 2).attr("y", captionY)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 13).attr("fill", "#333").text(st.caption);
    svg.append("text").attr("x", width - mR).attr("y", captionY)
      .attr("text-anchor", "end").attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#bbb")
      .text((f + 1) + "/" + total);
  }

  playBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = !playing; playBtn.text(playing ? "Pause" : "Play"); });
  resetBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { f = 0; update(); });
  prevBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; playBtn.text("Play"); f = (f - 1 + total) % total; update(); });
  nextBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; playBtn.text("Play"); f = (f + 1) % total; update(); });

  const timer = setInterval(() =&gt; { if (playing) { f = (f + 1) % total; update(); } }, 1300);
  invalidation.then(() =&gt; clearInterval(timer));

  update();
  return container.node();
}

3.2 VAR from a mathematical POV
A VAR is an \(n\)-equation, \(n\)-variable linear model in which each variable is explained by its own lagged values, plus current (initially this term will missed, it appears later in the text in Section 3.3.1) and past values of the remaining \(n-1\) variables.
Formally, let \(Z_t\) be a vector collecting the values of \(n\) variables at time \(t\), for instance inflation, output gap, and the interest rate. A vector autoregressive process of order \(p\), written VAR(\(p\)), generalizes the one-variable AR(\(p\)) process to \(n\) variables:
\[Z_t = c + \Phi_1 Z_{t-1} + \Phi_2 Z_{t-2} + \cdots + \Phi_p Z_{t-p} + w_t
\tag{3.1}\]
where:
\[Z_t = \begin{bmatrix} z_{1t} \\ z_{2t} \\ \vdots \\ z_{nt} \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
c = \begin{bmatrix} c_1 \\ c_2 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
\Phi_j = \begin{bmatrix} \phi_{11}^{(j)} & \cdots & \phi_{1n}^{(j)} \\ \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\ \phi_{n1}^{(j)} & \cdots & \phi_{nn}^{(j)} \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
w_t = \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} \\ w_{2t} \\ \vdots \\ w_{nt} \end{bmatrix}
\tag{3.2}\]
The model describes the evolution of \(Z_t\) as a function of its own past realizations \(Z_{t-1}, \dots, Z_{t-p}\) and of a vector of white noise errors \(w_t\).






NoteDeeper dive: How the system grows with n variable and p lags





The compact form of Equation 3.1 hides how quickly the system grows once \(n\) and \(p\) increase. The interactive below writes out the full set of equations for any chosen number of variables and lags (max 6 and 6), and tracks the number of parameters this entails.

{
  const katex = await require("katex@0.16.9");
  if (!document.querySelector("link[data-katex-css]")) {
    const link = document.createElement("link");
    link.rel = "stylesheet";
    link.href = "https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/KaTeX/0.16.9/katex.min.css";
    link.setAttribute("data-katex-css", "1");
    document.head.appendChild(link);
  }

  const maxN = 6, maxP = 6;
  const cCoef = "#2f6fb0", cConst = "#777", cShock = "#c0392b";

  const container = d3.create("div").style("font-family", "system-ui, sans-serif");

  const controls = container.append("div")
    .style("display", "flex").style("gap", "28px").style("align-items", "center")
    .style("flex-wrap", "wrap").style("margin-bottom", "18px");

  function stepper(label, min, max, initial) {
    const wrap = controls.append("div").style("display", "flex")
      .style("align-items", "center").style("gap", "10px");
    wrap.append("span").style("font-size", "13px").style("color", "#333")
      .style("font-weight", "600").text(label);
    const group = wrap.append("div").style("display", "flex").style("align-items", "center")
      .style("border", "1px solid #cfcfcf").style("border-radius", "8px")
      .style("overflow", "hidden").style("background", "#fff")
      .style("box-shadow", "inset 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.04)");
    const mk = (txt) =&gt; group.append("button").html(txt)
      .style("width", "30px").style("height", "30px").style("border", "none")
      .style("background", "#f7f7f7").style("cursor", "pointer")
      .style("font-size", "16px").style("line-height", "1").style("color", "#444")
      .style("display", "flex").style("align-items", "center").style("justify-content", "center");
    const minus = mk("\u2212");
    minus.style("border-right", "1px solid #e2e2e2");
    const val = group.append("span").style("min-width", "34px").style("text-align", "center")
      .style("font-size", "15px").style("font-weight", "700").style("color", "#222").text(initial);
    const plus = mk("+");
    plus.style("border-left", "1px solid #e2e2e2");
    let v = initial;
    const set = nv =&gt; { v = Math.max(min, Math.min(max, nv)); val.text(v); render(); };
    minus.on("click", () =&gt; set(v - 1));
    plus.on("click", () =&gt; set(v + 1));
    return { get: () =&gt; v };
  }

  const nCtrl = stepper("Variables (n)", 1, maxN, 2);
  const pCtrl = stepper("Lags (p)", 1, maxP, 1);

  const holder = container.append("div");

  function render() {
    const n = nCtrl.get(), p = pCtrl.get();
    holder.selectAll("*").remove();

    const rows = [];
    for (let i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
      let r = "\\mathbf{z_{" + i + ",t}} &= \\textcolor{" + cConst + "}{c_{" + i + "}}";
      for (let lag = 1; lag &lt;= p; lag++) {
        for (let k = 1; k &lt;= n; k++) {
          const sup = p &gt; 1 ? "^{(" + lag + ")}" : "";
          r += " + \\textcolor{" + cCoef + "}{\\phi_{" + i + k + "}" + sup + "}\\, z_{" + k + ",t-" + lag + "}";
        }
      }
      r += " + \\textcolor{" + cShock + "}{w_{" + i + ",t}}";
      rows.push(r);
    }
    const latex = "\\begin{aligned}" + rows.join(" \\\\[4pt] ") + "\\end{aligned}";

    const eqBox = holder.append("div")
      .style("background", "#fcfcfb").style("border", "1px solid #ececec")
      .style("border-radius", "10px").style("padding", "20px 22px")
      .style("overflow-x", "auto");
    eqBox.node().innerHTML = katex.renderToString(latex, { throwOnError: false, displayMode: true });

    const arCoef = p * n * n;
    const totalCoef = n + arCoef;

    const stat = holder.append("div")
      .style("margin-top", "16px").style("display", "flex").style("gap", "10px")
      .style("flex-wrap", "wrap");

    const card = (big, small, hot) =&gt; {
      const c = stat.append("div")
        .style("flex", "1").style("min-width", "150px")
        .style("background", hot ? "#fff7e6" : "#f5f7fa")
        .style("border", "1px solid " + (hot ? "#f0c667" : "#e2e8f0"))
        .style("border-radius", "8px").style("padding", "10px 12px");
      c.append("div").style("font-size", "22px").style("font-weight", "bold")
        .style("color", hot ? "#b8770a" : "#333").text(big);
      c.append("div").style("font-size", "11px").style("color", "#777").html(small);
    };

    card(arCoef, "AR coefficients&lt;br&gt;p\u00B7n\u00B2 = " + p + "\u00B7" + n + "\u00B2", true);
    card(totalCoef, "total coefficients&lt;br&gt;n + p\u00B7n\u00B2", false);
  }

  render();
  return container.node();
}






The parameter counts shown in the two boxes follow a simple rule. Each of the \(n\) equations carries a constant plus \(n\) coefficients per lag, plus the autoregressive part that grows as \(pn^2\): linearly in the lags, but quadratically in the variables, giving \(n + pn^2\) across the system.
So a modest VAR with \(n = 4\) and \(p = 4\) already needs \(4 + 4\cdot 16 = 69\) coefficients, estimated from macroeconomic samples that are rarely long.
This is the curse of dimensionality of VARs, and the reason parsimony in \(n\) and \(p\) is central to specification.




3.2.1 The error structure
The white-noise assumption on \(w_t\) is central and deserves to be stated precisely. Each error has zero mean and finite variance, is serially uncorrelated within equation over time but it may be correlated across equations at the same time. This distinction is crucial and recurs multiple time throughout the text.
Formally:
\[\operatorname{E}[w_t] = 0, \qquad
\operatorname{E}[w_t w_\tau'] =
\begin{cases}
\Omega & \text{if } t = \tau \\
0 & \text{if } t \neq \tau
\end{cases}
\tag{3.3}\]
More precisely:

Along the time dimension, the errors are uncorrelated with their own past and with all the right-hand-side variables, the lagged \(Z\)’s. This absence of serial correlation is what makes OLS, applied equation by equation, consistent.
Across equations, by contrast, \(\Omega\) is not assumed to be diagonal6, so the errors of different equations can be correlated at the same date \(t\). The name reduced-form comes precisely from this: Equation 3.1 is what remains after the contemporaneous variables have been solved out of the equations, and the non-zero off-diagonal of \(\Omega\) is the trace they leave behind.

This cross-equation correlation is not a nuisance to be removed but the core point of the reduced-form.

The general picture can now begin to take shape. Contemporaneous links between variables are initially absent from the equations, even though, it is clear and intuitive, that macroeconomic variables influence one another within the same period. For example, assuming that GDP and inflation are mutually inert over a quarter would be unreliable. Their absence from the right-hand side is therefore not a claim about how the real world works but a condition for estimating the model at all, and this is the key point around which the whole chapter is organized.
The demonstration comes later. For the moment two facts can be taken as given: contemporaneous variables are kept off the right-hand side because their presence would make the model impossible to estimate, and it is precisely this absence that makes what is estimated a reduced-form. Everything the contemporaneous structure still has to say is now concentrated in one object, \(\Omega\), which deserves examination on its own.

3.2.1.1 The error variance covariance matrix
The matrix \(\Omega\) introduced in Equation 3.3 is the contemporaneous variance-covariance matrix of the error vector \(w_t\). Its entries are what needs to be examined: the diagonal and the off-diagonal carry different information, and seeing why, from the algebra, clarifies what the matrix actually measures.
Its content follows directly from the definition \(\Omega = \operatorname{E}[w_t w_t']\). To see why it holds variances on the diagonal and covariances off it, take \(n = 2\) and expand.
The outer product of the error vector with itself is:
\[w_t w_t' = \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} \\ w_{2t} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} & w_{2t} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t}^2 & w_{1t} w_{2t} \\ w_{2t} w_{1t} & w_{2t}^2 \end{bmatrix},\]
so taking expectations entry by entry gives
\[\Omega = \begin{bmatrix} \operatorname{E}[w_{1t}^2] & \operatorname{E}[w_{1t} w_{2t}] \\ \operatorname{E}[w_{2t} w_{1t}] & \operatorname{E}[w_{2t}^2] \end{bmatrix}.\]
The white-noise assumption fixes what each entry is. Because the errors have zero mean, the general definitions of variance and covariance simplify:
\[\operatorname{Var}(w_{it}) = \operatorname{E}[w_{it}^2] - \underbrace{(\operatorname{E}[w_{it}])^2}_{=\,0} = \operatorname{E}[w_{it}^2], \qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(w_{it}, w_{jt}) = \operatorname{E}[w_{it} w_{jt}] - \underbrace{\operatorname{E}[w_{it}]\operatorname{E}[w_{jt}]}_{=\,0} = \operatorname{E}[w_{it} w_{jt}].\]
The diagonal entries \(\operatorname{E}[w_{it}^2]\) are therefore the variances \(\sigma_i^2\), and the off-diagonal entries \(\operatorname{E}[w_{it} w_{jt}]\) the contemporaneous covariances \(\sigma_{ij}\).
Written out, for clarification, for \(n = 2\) and \(n = 3\) the matrix takes the following form:
\[\Omega_{(n=2)} = \begin{bmatrix} \sigma_1^2 & \sigma_{12} \\ \sigma_{12} & \sigma_2^2 \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\Omega_{(n=3)} = \begin{bmatrix} \sigma_1^2 & \sigma_{12} & \sigma_{13} \\ \sigma_{12} & \sigma_2^2 & \sigma_{23} \\ \sigma_{13} & \sigma_{23} & \sigma_3^2 \end{bmatrix}
\tag{3.4}\]
Two things can be read from this structure directly. The diagonal collects the variances \(\sigma_i^2\), one per equation, fixing the scale of each equation’s own error; the off-diagonal collects the contemporaneous covariances \(\sigma_{ij}\), recording whether and in which direction the errors of equations \(i\) and \(j\) move together at the same date.
The matrix is symmetric, since \(\sigma_{ij} = \sigma_{ji}\), so the co-movement between two equations is a single number, not two. A diagonal \(\Omega\), with every \(\sigma_{ij} = 0\), would describe a system whose errors are mutually uncorrelated, each equation disturbed on its own; in macroeconomic data this is the exception rather than the rule.
A non-zero off-diagonal is thus the footprint of the contemporaneous links among the variables. As long as non-zero values are present, \(w_t\) cannot be called a vector of structural shocks: the terms used instead are innovations, reduced-form residuals, or one-step-ahead forecast errors of the system. Structural shocks require the off-diagonal entries to be zero (called orthogonality7), since only then would the errors be mutually uncorrelated and thus separately interpretable.
Separate interpretability is what the whole exercise rests on. Tracing the effect of a shock means asking how the system reacts when one disturbance moves and everything else stays still, the usual ceteris paribus clause. Correlated errors make that question unanswerable: if \(w_{1t}\) and \(w_{2t}\) move together, a movement in the first never occurs alone, and what is observed is the joint response to both, not the isolated effect of one. Only orthogonal shocks can be moved one at a time, and only then does the question have a well-defined answer. Reaching them starting from reduced-form residuals is the goal of identification: Chapter 5 examines what structural shocks are used for and the ways of recovering them.






NoteDeeper dive: How the variance-covariance matrix grows with n variables and p lags





The interactive below writes out \(\Omega\) for a chosen number of variables and lags. Raising \(n\) enlarges the matrix; raising \(p\) leaves it untouched.

{
  const katex = await require("katex@0.16.9");
  if (!document.querySelector("link[data-katex-css]")) {
    const link = document.createElement("link");
    link.rel = "stylesheet";
    link.href = "https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/KaTeX/0.16.9/katex.min.css";
    link.setAttribute("data-katex-css", "1");
    document.head.appendChild(link);
  }

  const maxN = 6, maxP = 6;
  const cVar = "#b8770a", cCov = "#2f6fb0";

  const container = d3.create("div").style("font-family", "system-ui, sans-serif");

  const controls = container.append("div")
    .style("display", "flex").style("gap", "28px").style("align-items", "center")
    .style("flex-wrap", "wrap").style("margin-bottom", "18px");

  function stepper(label, min, max, initial) {
    const wrap = controls.append("div").style("display", "flex")
      .style("align-items", "center").style("gap", "10px");
    wrap.append("span").style("font-size", "13px").style("color", "#333")
      .style("font-weight", "600").text(label);
    const group = wrap.append("div").style("display", "flex").style("align-items", "center")
      .style("border", "1px solid #cfcfcf").style("border-radius", "8px")
      .style("overflow", "hidden").style("background", "#fff")
      .style("box-shadow", "inset 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.04)");
