4.1 From chapter 3 to chapter 4, the bridge:
What happens to output and prices when a demand shock hits? How far do inflation and employment move after a monetary tightening, and for how long? What does a sudden rise in oil prices do to the rest of the system? These are the questions macroeconometrics is built to answer.
The reasons for building a VAR at all, beyond data description and forecasting, are structural inference (“What is the causal effect of a shock on a variable?”) and policy analysis (“What would happen if the policy maker were to follow a different rule?”), and both require the ability to trace how variables respond to a shock.
Chapter 3 showed why the reduced form cannot answer them as it stands. It is estimable, but its residuals are correlated: moving one moves the others as well, so no ceteris paribus interpretation is available and the effect of a single shock can never be isolated. A demand shock cannot be spoken of on its own, and the same holds for others. Recovering the structural shocks from the reduced-form ones is the task of identification, taken up in Chapter 5
Before that, this chapter builds the machinery that makes it possible to read the effect of a shock on macroeconomic variables. The VAR model is rewritten so that every value of the system appears as a weighted sum of past shocks, the weights being exactly the trajectory a single shock traces over time. Once that object is in hand, the reason identification matters becomes concrete: the trajectory is only economically meaningful if the shock being traced is a structural one.
Example:
To get a clear initial idea of what is going to be discussed, see the mechanism at work. Consider a bivariate system in the spirit of the previous chapter, with the output gap and inflation each depending on their own lag and on the other’s lag. Everything is in equilibrium, all variables sitting at their long-run values, until a single shock arrives at horizon zero and hits one equation.
Following what happens next makes the propagation of a shock across a (simple) interconnected system visible. At impact only the variable that was hit moves. One period later the disturbance has reached the other equation through the cross-lag, and from there it travels back, since the second variable feeds the first in turn. What began as a one-off disturbance to one equation becomes a trajectory for the whole system, and the interactive below traces it horizon by horizon.
The four sliders control the coefficients of the two equations: the two own coefficients govern how long each variable holds on to its own past, the two cross coefficients how strongly each equation feeds the other.
{
  const katex = await require("katex@0.16.9");
  if (!document.querySelector("link[data-katex-css]")) {
    const l = document.createElement("link");
    l.rel = "stylesheet"; l.href = "https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/KaTeX/0.16.9/katex.min.css";
    l.setAttribute("data-katex-css", "1"); document.head.appendChild(l);
  }

  const SANS = "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif";
  const C1 = "#2f6fb0", C2 = "#c0392b", AMBER = "#a8760f";
  const H = 16, HOLD = 3;              // HOLD extra ticks on impact
  const TOTAL = HOLD + H + 4;

  let phi11 = 0.7, phi22 = 0.6, phi21 = 0.35, phi12 = -0.15;
  let shockOn = 0, tick = 0, playing = true;

  const root = d3.create("div").style("font-family", SANS);

  // ---- equations ------------------------------------------------------
  const eqBox = root.append("div")
    .style("background", "#fcfcfb").style("border", "1px solid #ececec")
    .style("border-radius", "10px").style("padding", "14px 18px")
    .style("margin-bottom", "12px").style("overflow-x", "auto");

  function drawEq() {
    const t = (v, col) =&gt; "\\textcolor{" + col + "}{" +
      (v &gt;= 0 ? "+\\," : "-\\,") + Math.abs(v).toFixed(2) + "}";
    const lead = (v, col) =&gt; "\\textcolor{" + col + "}{" +
      (v &gt;= 0 ? "" : "-\\,") + Math.abs(v).toFixed(2) + "}";
    const r1 = "y_t &= " + lead(phi11, C1) + "\\, y_{t-1} " + t(phi12, C2)
             + "\\, \\pi_{t-1} + \\textcolor{" + C1 + "}{w_{y,t}}";
    const r2 = "\\pi_t &= " + lead(phi21, C1) + "\\, y_{t-1} " + t(phi22, C2)
             + "\\, \\pi_{t-1} + \\textcolor{" + C2 + "}{w_{\\pi,t}}";
    eqBox.node().innerHTML = katex.renderToString(
      "\\begin{aligned}" + r1 + " \\\\[5pt] " + r2 + "\\end{aligned}",
      { throwOnError: false, displayMode: true });
  }

  // ---- controls -------------------------------------------------------
  const bar = root.append("div").style("display", "flex").style("gap", "8px")
    .style("align-items", "center").style("margin-bottom", "10px").style("flex-wrap", "wrap");
  const mkBtn = t =&gt; bar.append("button").text(t)
    .style("padding", "4px 12px").style("font-size", "13px")
    .style("border", "1px solid #ccc").style("border-radius", "6px")
    .style("background", "#fff").style("cursor", "pointer");
  const playBtn = mkBtn("Pause");
  mkBtn("Reset").on("click", () =&gt; { tick = 0; render(); });

  bar.append("span").style("width", "12px");
  bar.append("span").style("font-size", "13px").style("font-weight", "600")
    .style("color", "#333").text("Shock hits:");
  const pick = bar.append("div").style("display", "flex")
    .style("border", "1px solid #cfcfcf").style("border-radius", "8px")
    .style("overflow", "hidden").style("background", "#fff");
  const pickBtns = ["output gap", "inflation"].map((lab, i) =&gt; {
    const b = pick.append("button").text(lab)
      .style("padding", "5px 14px").style("border", "none").style("cursor", "pointer")
      .style("font-size", "12.5px");
    if (i &gt; 0) b.style("border-left", "1px solid #e2e2e2");
    b.on("click", () =&gt; { shockOn = i; tick = 0; paintPick(); render(); });
    return b;
  });
  const paintPick = () =&gt; pickBtns.forEach((b, i) =&gt;
    b.style("background", shockOn === i ? "#eef3fa" : "#fff")
     .style("color", shockOn === i ? "#1f4e79" : "#666")
     .style("font-weight", shockOn === i ? "700" : "400"));
  paintPick();

  // ---- sliders --------------------------------------------------------
  const sl = root.append("div").style("display", "grid")
    .style("grid-template-columns", "repeat(2, minmax(220px, 1fr))")
    .style("gap", "6px 26px").style("margin-bottom", "14px");

  function slider(label, init, set) {
    const row = sl.append("div").style("display", "flex")
      .style("align-items", "center").style("gap", "9px");
    const tag = row.append("span").style("font-size", "12px").style("min-width", "104px");
    tag.node().innerHTML = katex.renderToString(label, { throwOnError: false });
    const inp = row.append("input").attr("type", "range")
      .attr("min", "-1.5").attr("max", "1.5").attr("step", "0.05")
      .attr("value", init).style("flex", "1").style("min-width", "80px");
    const out = row.append("span").style("font-size", "12px").style("font-weight", "700")
      .style("min-width", "40px").style("color", "#222").text((+init).toFixed(2));
    inp.on("input", () =&gt; {
      const v = +inp.property("value");
      out.text(v.toFixed(2)); set(v); drawEq(); render();
    });
  }
  slider("\\phi_{11}\\ \\text{(own)}", phi11, v =&gt; phi11 = v);
  slider("\\phi_{12}\\ \\text{(cross)}", phi12, v =&gt; phi12 = v);
  slider("\\phi_{21}\\ \\text{(cross)}", phi21, v =&gt; phi21 = v);
  slider("\\phi_{22}\\ \\text{(own)}", phi22, v =&gt; phi22 = v);

  const holder = root.append("div");

  function paths() {
    let P = [[1, 0], [0, 1]];
    const a = [], b = [];
    for (let j = 0; j &lt;= H; j++) {
      a.push(P[0][shockOn]); b.push(P[1][shockOn]);
      P = [
        [phi11 * P[0][0] + phi12 * P[1][0], phi11 * P[0][1] + phi12 * P[1][1]],
        [phi21 * P[0][0] + phi22 * P[1][0], phi21 * P[0][1] + phi22 * P[1][1]]
      ];
    }
    return [a, b];
  }

  const fmt = v =&gt; {
    const m = Math.abs(v);
    if (m === 0) return "0";
    if (m &gt;= 1000) return d3.format(".2s")(v);
    if (m &gt;= 10) return d3.format(".0f")(v);
    return d3.format(".2f")(v);
  };

  function render() {
    holder.selectAll("*").remove();
    const [ra, rb] = paths();
    const shown = Math.min(Math.max(0, tick - HOLD), H);
    const atImpact = tick &lt;= HOLD;

    const W = 700, ht = 330, mL = 74, mR = 96, mB = 46, mT = 40;
    const dx = (W - mL - mR) / H;
    const plotH = ht - mT - mB;
    const zero = mT + plotH / 2;
    const peak = Math.max(1e-6, d3.max(ra.concat(rb).map(Math.abs)));
    const dom = peak * 1.12;
    const sc = (plotH / 2) / dom;
    const X = j =&gt; mL + j * dx, Y = v =&gt; zero - v * sc;

    const svg = holder.append("svg").attr("viewBox", [0, 0, W, ht])
      .attr("width", W).attr("style", "max-width:100%;height:auto;");

    const ticks = d3.scaleLinear().domain([-dom, dom]).ticks(7);
    ticks.forEach(v =&gt; {
      svg.append("line").attr("x1", mL - 6).attr("x2", W - mR + 6)
        .attr("y1", Y(v)).attr("y2", Y(v))
        .attr("stroke", Math.abs(v) &lt; 1e-12 ? "#c9c9c9" : "#f1f1f1");
      svg.append("text").attr("x", mL - 12).attr("y", Y(v) + 4)
        .attr("text-anchor", "end").attr("font-size", 10.5).attr("fill", "#999")
        .attr("font-family", SANS).text(fmt(v));
    });
    svg.append("line").attr("x1", mL).attr("x2", mL)
      .attr("y1", mT).attr("y2", ht - mB).attr("stroke", "#e2e2e2");
    svg.append("text").attr("x", 14).attr("y", zero)
      .attr("transform", "rotate(-90 14 " + zero + ")").attr("text-anchor", "middle")
      .attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#999").attr("font-family", SANS)
      .text("response to a unit shock");

    const ends = [];
    [[ra, C1, "output gap"], [rb, C2, "inflation"]].forEach(([r, col, lab]) =&gt; {
      const seg = r.slice(0, shown + 1);
      svg.append("path").attr("d", d3.line().x((d, j) =&gt; X(j)).y(d =&gt; Y(d))
        .curve(d3.curveMonotoneX)(seg))
        .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", col).attr("stroke-width", 2.4)
        .attr("opacity", 0.9);
      seg.forEach((v, j) =&gt; svg.append("circle").attr("cx", X(j)).attr("cy", Y(v))
        .attr("r", j === shown ? 4.5 : 2.4).attr("fill", col)
        .attr("opacity", j === shown ? 1 : 0.5));
      ends.push({ y: Y(seg[seg.length - 1]), col, lab });
    });

    ends.sort((p, q) =&gt; p.y - q.y);
    if (ends[1].y - ends[0].y &lt; 15) {
      const mid = (ends[0].y + ends[1].y) / 2;
      ends[0].y = mid - 8; ends[1].y = mid + 8;
    }
    ends.forEach(e =&gt; svg.append("text").attr("x", X(shown) + 10).attr("y", e.y + 4)
      .attr("font-size", 11.5).attr("font-weight", "bold").attr("fill", e.col)
      .attr("font-family", SANS).text(e.lab));

    d3.range(0, H + 1, 2).forEach(j =&gt;
      svg.append("text").attr("x", X(j)).attr("y", ht - mB + 18)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#999")
        .attr("font-family", SANS).text(j));
    svg.append("text").attr("x", (W - mR + mL) / 2).attr("y", ht - 8)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 11.5).attr("fill", "#888")
      .attr("font-family", SANS).text("periods after the shock");

    // ---- impact marker: pulsing rings + named label -------------------
    if (atImpact) {
      const col = shockOn === 0 ? C1 : C2;
      const cx = X(0), cy = Y(1);
      for (let k = 0; k &lt; 3; k++) {
        const ring = svg.append("circle").attr("cx", cx).attr("cy", cy).attr("r", 6)
          .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", col).attr("stroke-width", 2.5);
        ring.append("animate").attr("attributeName", "r")
          .attr("values", "6;26").attr("dur", "1.4s")
          .attr("begin", (k * 0.46) + "s").attr("repeatCount", "indefinite");
        ring.append("animate").attr("attributeName", "opacity")
          .attr("values", "0.9;0").attr("dur", "1.4s")
          .attr("begin", (k * 0.46) + "s").attr("repeatCount", "indefinite");
      }
      svg.append("circle").attr("cx", cx).attr("cy", cy).attr("r", 6.5).attr("fill", col);
      const lab = svg.append("text").attr("x", cx + 14).attr("y", cy - 16)
        .attr("font-size", 13).attr("font-weight", "bold").attr("fill", AMBER)
        .attr("font-family", SANS)
        .text((shockOn === 0 ? "output gap" : "inflation") + " shock hits");
      lab.append("animate").attr("attributeName", "opacity")
        .attr("values", "1;0.35;1").attr("dur", "1.4s").attr("repeatCount", "indefinite");
    }

    const other = shockOn === 0 ? "inflation" : "the output gap";
    const hit = shockOn === 0 ? "the output gap" : "inflation";
    const cross = shockOn === 0 ? phi21 : phi12;
    const note = shown === 0
      ? "At impact only " + hit + " moves: the shock enters one equation, and nothing has yet passed to the other."
      : Math.abs(cross) &lt; 0.03
      ? "With the cross-coefficient near zero, " + other + " barely reacts: the system is almost two separate models."
      : "One period later " + other + " has moved too, through the cross-lag.";

    holder.append("div").style("margin-top", "8px")
      .style("font-size", "12.5px").style("color", "#666").style("line-height", "1.6")
      .text("Horizon " + shown + " of " + H + ". " + note);
  }

  playBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = !playing; playBtn.text(playing ? "Pause" : "Play"); });
  const timer = setInterval(() =&gt; {
    if (playing) { tick = tick &gt;= TOTAL ? 0 : tick + 1; render(); }
  }, 700);
  invalidation.then(() =&gt; clearInterval(timer));

  drawEq(); render();
  return root.node();
}
Three things are worth drawing out of this picture.
Try pushing the coefficients up. When they are small the two lines bend back towards the zero line, the shock fades and the system returns to equilibrium without diverging; when they are large the lines drift away instead and the response keeps growing. This is exactly the notion of stationarity, which is set out formally later in the chapter. Watch the vertical scale while doing this, since the chart rescales itself.

4.3 Part A - The scalar case: AR(1)

4.3.1 Recursive substitution
Starting with a first-order autoregressive process (the constant is dropped, since it would only shift the mean), with \(w_t\) the classic white-noise shock:
\[
y_t = \phi\, y_{t-1} + w_t,
\qquad w_t \sim \text{WN}(0,\sigma^2)
\tag{4.1}\]
The idea is to rewrite \(y_t\) in another form, by substituting the process repeatedly into itself. The model holds at every date, so \(y_{t-1}\) can itself be written in terms of \(y_{t-2}\), and \(y_{t-2}\) in terms of \(y_{t-3}\), and so on:
\[
y_{t-1} = \phi\, y_{t-2} + w_{t-1}, \qquad
y_{t-2} = \phi\, y_{t-3} + w_{t-2}, \qquad \dots
\]
Each substitution moves one period further back: a further shock enters the expression and the power of \(\phi\) rises by one, step after step, until the chain reaches the initial value \(y_{-1}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
y_t &= \phi\, y_{t-1} + w_t \\[2pt]
    &= \phi\,\underbrace{(\phi\, y_{t-2} + w_{t-1})}_{y_{t-1}} + w_t
       = \phi^{2} y_{t-2} + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t \\[2pt]
    &= \phi^{2}\underbrace{(\phi\, y_{t-3} + w_{t-2})}_{y_{t-2}} + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t
       = \phi^{3} y_{t-3} + \phi^{2}w_{t-2} + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t \\[2pt]
    &\;\;\vdots \\[2pt]
    &= \phi^{\,t+1} y_{-1} + \phi^{\,t}w_0 + \cdots + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.2}\]
